+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-content">
+        <p>
+          Every unforgettable cup of coffee begins long before the first crack in our roasting drum. At The Daily Grind, our bean sourcing process is rooted in multi-year, direct-trade partnerships with smallholder farmers and cooperatives across the world's premier coffee-growing belt—from the misty highlands of Yirgacheffe, Ethiopia, to the volcanic slopes of Huehuetenango, Guatemala, and the lush Andean valleys of Huila, Colombia. By working directly with producers rather than relying on anonymous commodity brokers, we ensure complete traceability down to the individual farm plot while paying premiums well above Fair Trade minimums so farming communities can reinvest in their land and families.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Exceptional flavor requires patience and biodiversity at origin. We exclusively partner with farms practicing shade-grown, regenerative agriculture at elevations between 1,400 and 2,100 meters above sea level. Cooler mountain nights slow the maturation of the coffee cherry, allowing complex sugars and organic acids to develop tightly within the seed. During harvest season, skilled pickers walk the steep rows multiple times, selectively hand-harvesting only peak-ripe, deep-crimson cherries while leaving under-ripe fruit on the branch for later passes.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Once harvested, cherries are brought to local micro-mills within hours to prevent uncontrolled fermentation. Depending on the varietal and regional climate, our partner producers employ meticulously monitored washed, honey, or natural sun-dried processing methods. Washed lots are fermented in clean mountain spring water and dried on raised African beds, yielding bright, tea-like clarity and floral aromatics. Honey and natural lots are turned by hand every hour on sunlit patios to cultivate rich notes of stone fruit, dark berries, and caramelized cane sugar while conserving up to eighty percent more water.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Before a single lot is approved for shipment, our green coffee buyers and certified Q-Graders conduct rigorous sensory cupping and physical analysis both at origin and in our roastery lab. Every sample must score 86 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale, remain free of primary defects, and stabilize at an ideal moisture content between 10.5% and 11.5%. Approved parchment coffee is rested, milled, and sealed inside hermetic GrainPro liners within jute sacks to lock in freshness during ocean transit, arriving at our roastery ready to be small-batch roasted to highlight every nuance of its origin.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+    </section>
